{"generation_id":"8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3","teaching_plan":{"arc":"The lesson opens on the child standing between the bright lamp and the wall and asks learners to commit to where the dark patch will land before anything is explained. It then builds the blocked-light mechanism — straight-line rays from the lamp are stopped by the opaque child, leaving a shadow on the wall on the far side of the child from the lamp — and closes by having each learner apply that model alone to a fresh opaque-object-and-lamp case.","revision":1,"sections":[{"blocks":[{"id":"orient-b1","brief":"Present the concrete scene from the anchor: a child standing between a bright lamp and a wall, with the lamp on one side of the child and the wall on the other. Name only the three parts — lamp, child, wall — and state that a dark patch appears somewhere. Do not explain why the dark patch forms, do not yet call it a shadow, and do not say which side it falls on. Close by asking the learner to commit to where they think the dark patch will appear: on the wall behind the child, on the lamp side of the child, or underneath the child.","intent":"orient","evidence":"The objective asks why a shadow forms and where it sits; an unexplained concrete scene gives the learner a specific case whose location question is precisely what the lesson must answer.","position":0,"evidence_refs":["anchor.anchor-1","slot.orient.purpose","lesson.objective"],"learner_action":{"action":"select-one","target":"which surface the dark patch will appear on in the child-lamp-wall scene","purpose":"elicit the learner's current spatial expectation before any explanation so the lesson can address it","difficulty":"guided","expected_evidence":"a committed prediction that reveals whether the learner already expects the dark patch on the far side of the child from the lamp"},"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":[],"stimulus_dependencies":["anchor.anchor-1"]},{"id":"orient-b2","brief":"Recall that light from a source such as a lamp travels in straight lines and spreads outward as it goes. Use the lamp in the child scene to make this concrete: light leaves the lamp and travels outward in straight lines toward the child and the wall. Do not yet say what happens when the light meets the child's body, and do not use the word shadow. The purpose is only to make the straight-line travel of light available for the next section.","intent":"activate-prior-knowledge","evidence":"The causal explanation that follows depends entirely on light travelling in straight lines; without that prior available, the blocking mechanism would have no link between lamp and wall.","position":1,"evidence_refs":["prior-1","prior-2","prior-4","anchor.anchor-1"],"learner_action":null,"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":[],"stimulus_dependencies":[]}],"slot_id":"orient","transition":null,"specific_purpose":"Open attention with the child-lamp-wall scene, surface the learner's current expectation about where the dark patch lands, and pull forward that light travels in straight lines so the explanation has a foundation."},{"blocks":[{"id":"explain-b1","brief":"Return to the child-lamp-wall scene. Follow the straight-line rays leaving the lamp toward the child and state that the child's body is opaque, so the light that would have reached the part of the wall behind the child does not get there. State the target idea precisely: the shadow is the region on the wall where light was blocked, not an object or substance the child gives off. The child does nothing to make a shadow; the shadow is what is missing from the light on the far side of the child from the lamp.","intent":"explain-cause","evidence":"The objective names both why a shadow appears and where it sits; the causal chain from lamp through straight-line travel to a blocked region on the far surface is exactly that explanation and cannot be reduced to a definition.","position":0,"evidence_refs":["lesson.objective","anchor.anchor-1","prior-1","prior-5","slot.explain.purpose"],"learner_action":null,"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":[],"stimulus_dependencies":["anchor.anchor-1"]},{"id":"explain-b2","brief":"Show the arrangement of the child-lamp-wall scene so the spatial relationship is visible: the lamp on one side, the opaque child in the middle, and the wall on the other side. Mark the region on the wall directly behind the child where the straight-line rays are stopped, and mark the lit region on the wall where rays pass the child and land. Label the three parts — lamp, child, wall — and label the dark region as the shadow. The relationship the learner must notice is that the shadow sits on the opposite side of the child from the lamp, not beside or under the child.","intent":"show-structure","evidence":"The objective requires describing the shadow's position relative to the source; a static arrangement of lamp, opaque child, and wall is the only way to show the opposite-side relationship that prose alone leaves ambiguous.","position":1,"evidence_refs":["lesson.objective","anchor.anchor-1","prior-1"],"learner_action":null,"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":[],"stimulus_dependencies":["anchor.anchor-1"]}],"slot_id":"explain","transition":"The previous section left the learner holding a committed prediction and a clear picture of light travelling straight from the lamp, so this section can now show exactly where those rays are stopped and where the resulting dark region falls.","specific_purpose":"Build the target idea: a shadow is the region on the far side of an opaque object where light that would have reached the surface was blocked, and its position follows from the straight-line path from the source."},{"blocks":[{"id":"contrast-b1","brief":"Keep the same lamp-and-wall setup. In the first case the child's opaque body blocks all the light, so a clear shadow falls on the wall opposite the lamp. In the second case the child is replaced by a translucent sheet held in the same place: some light passes through, so only a faint shadow appears, still on the wall opposite the lamp. Point out that the shadow's position does not move — it is still on the far side of the object from the lamp — and that what changes is how dark the shadow is, because the sheet blocks less light than the child.","intent":"compare","evidence":"The boundary the learner must see is between blocking all light and blocking some light; the same lamp-and-wall setup controls everything except the material, so the difference in shadow strength is the only change to read.","position":0,"evidence_refs":["prior-5","prior-7","anchor.anchor-1","slot.contrast.purpose"],"learner_action":null,"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":[],"stimulus_dependencies":["anchor.anchor-1"]},{"id":"contrast-b2","brief":"Surface the belief that the object itself makes or gives off the shadow. Ask the learner to respond to the claim: 'The child makes a shadow the way a lamp makes light.' A learner holding the misconception will agree; a learner holding the target model will reject it because the shadow is the absence of light on the far side of the child, not something the child produces. Then state the target model: the child does not create a shadow, it blocks light, and the shadow is what is left missing on the wall.","intent":"diagnose-misconception","evidence":"The approved misconception is a held belief, not a slip, and only a learner holding it would confidently agree that a shadow is something the object makes; a wrong answer here identifies that model directly.","position":1,"evidence_refs":["anchor.anchor-1","prior-5"],"learner_action":{"action":"select-one","target":"whether the child makes a shadow or blocks light to leave a shadow","purpose":"surface the wrong model that the object produces the shadow, so it can be replaced by the blocked-light model","difficulty":"guided","expected_evidence":"a commitment that reveals whether the learner treats the shadow as an emission from the object or as a region of blocked light"},"departure_reason":"The contrast slot's purpose is to place a case and a near-miss side by side so the learner sees the boundary of the idea; a held wrong belief about what produces a shadow is exactly that near-miss and must be surfaced, not merely flagged.","source_question_ids":["8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:c7bf440d-f1da-4edc-8731-07d34d206183.i2"],"stimulus_dependencies":["anchor.anchor-1"]},{"id":"contrast-b3","brief":"Surface the belief that the shadow falls on the same side of the object as the lamp. Reuse the lamp-and-wall setup and ask the learner to respond to the claim: 'The dark patch appears between the lamp and the child, on the lamp's side.' A learner holding the misconception will agree or be unsure; a learner holding the target model will place the shadow on the wall on the opposite side of the child from the lamp. Then restate the target: because light travels straight from the lamp past the child, it is stopped on the far side, so the shadow lands on the wall behind the child, opposite the lamp.","intent":"diagnose-misconception","evidence":"The objective explicitly requires describing the shadow's position relative to the source, and this approved misconception is the wrong position; surfacing it here tests that spatial model before the independent check.","position":2,"evidence_refs":["anchor.anchor-1","prior-1","lesson.objective"],"learner_action":{"action":"select-one","target":"which side of the child relative to the lamp the dark patch appears on","purpose":"surface the wrong-side misconception so it can be replaced by the blocked-light and opposite-side model","difficulty":"guided","expected_evidence":"a committed position that reveals whether the learner places the shadow between the lamp and the object or behind the object opposite the lamp"},"departure_reason":"The contrast slot must show the boundary of the idea by placing the target case beside a near-miss; the same-side belief is the near-miss for the positional half of the objective and cannot be left untested.","source_question_ids":["8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:c7bf440d-f1da-4edc-8731-07d34d206183.i3"],"stimulus_dependencies":["anchor.anchor-1"]}],"slot_id":"contrast","transition":"The learner now holds the model that a shadow is a blocked-light region on the far side of an opaque object; the next step sharpens that idea by showing what happens when the object in the same setup is not fully opaque and by testing the two wrong beliefs directly.","specific_purpose":"Put the opaque child and a translucent sheet in the same lamp-and-wall setup so the learner sees the boundary of the idea: a shadow is not a property of the object but a consequence of how much light it blocks. Confront the beliefs that the object makes the shadow and that the shadow lands on the lamp's side."},{"blocks":[{"id":"check-b1","brief":"Present a new case that is not the child scene: an opaque object stands between a lamp and a screen, with the lamp on one side and the screen on the other. The learner must select the correct explanation of where the shadow appears and why, using the model that light travels in straight lines from the lamp, the opaque object blocks that light, and the shadow is the blocked-light region on the screen on the far side of the object from the lamp. Do not repeat the child scenario, and do not restate the model before the learner responds.","intent":"practise-independent","evidence":"The check must show whether the learner can apply the blocked-light and opposite-side model unaided on a case that is not the anchor, which is exactly what independent rehearsal with the approved source provides.","position":0,"evidence_refs":["lesson.objective","slot.check.purpose","prior-1","prior-5","anchor.anchor-1"],"learner_action":{"action":"select-one","target":"where the shadow appears and why for the new opaque-object-and-lamp case","purpose":"measure whether the learner can apply the blocked-light and opposite-side model without support","difficulty":"independent","expected_evidence":"a correct selection that names the blocked-light region on the far side of the opaque object from the lamp"},"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":["8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:c7bf440d-f1da-4edc-8731-07d34d206183.i1"],"stimulus_dependencies":[]}],"slot_id":"check","transition":"The learner now holds the model that a shadow is a region of blocked light on the far side of an opaque object from the source, having seen it applied to the child and contrasted with a translucent sheet; the close asks for that model to be used alone on a new case.","specific_purpose":"Have the learner independently apply the blocked-light and opposite-side model to a fresh opaque-object-and-lamp case, producing evidence that the objective has landed without the anchor scene in front of them."}],"anchor_usage":[{"usage":"Introduce the child-lamp-wall scene as the concrete situation and elicit a commitment about where the dark patch appears.","slot_id":"orient"},{"usage":"Return to the child to follow straight-line rays from the lamp, show the opaque body stopping them, and locate the shadow on the wall opposite the lamp.","slot_id":"explain"},{"usage":"Reuse the same lamp-and-wall setup with the child replaced by a translucent sheet so the learner sees that shadow strength depends on how much light is blocked, not on the object making a shadow.","slot_id":"contrast"},{"usage":"Present a new opaque-object-and-lamp case without the child scene, so the model is applied without the anchor in front of the learner.","slot_id":"check"}],"approval_status":"approved","preparation_hash":"a9530874d702efc409dcd4c87658fe037981b8a8a6ed76e1d95a551ff8885870","teaching_plan_id":"2b32d711-86a6-460c-878d-4e59c4c3e159","misconception_focus_ids":["shadow-is-made-by-the-object","shadow-on-same-side-as-light"]},"teaching_validation":{"ok":true,"issues":[]},"teaching_qc":[],"teaching_review":{"status":"approved","revision":2,"reviewed_at":"2026-09-13T11:54:47Z","reviewed_by":"2f297058-f6bc-47bf-9ffd-3208ad8246d4","teacher_note":"Approved","approved_revision":1},"lesson_packet":{"scope":{"terminology":[],"must_establish":[],"must_not_introduce":[]},"slots":[{"purpose":"Give the learner a reason to care and a concrete situation to hold.","slot_id":"orient","max_blocks":2,"min_blocks":1,"typical_intents":["orient","activate-prior-knowledge","emphasise"],"visual_required":false},{"purpose":"Build the idea itself. State it, ground it, make it usable.","slot_id":"explain","max_blocks":3,"min_blocks":1,"typical_intents":["explain-cause","explain","define","show-structure","trace-flow","compare","name-parts"],"visual_required":false},{"purpose":"Put a case and a near-miss side by side. The learner must see the boundary of the idea, not just the idea.\n","slot_id":"contrast","max_blocks":3,"min_blocks":1,"typical_intents":["compare","classify","explain","warn","show-structure"],"visual_required":false},{"purpose":"The shared diagnostic. Identical across every variant. Written from the card only, never from the prose above it.\n","slot_id":"check","max_blocks":1,"min_blocks":1,"typical_intents":["check-understanding","practise-independent","diagnose-misconception"],"visual_required":false}],"anchor":{"id":"anchor-1","description":"A child standing between a bright lamp and a wall, where their body blocks the lamp light and a dar…"},"lesson":{"subject":"Science","objective":"Explain why a shadow appears when an opaque object blocks light, and describe the position of the shadow relative to the light source","grade_level":"Grade","lesson_mode":"first_exposure","knowledge_type":"conceptual","path_lesson_id":"8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3"},"limits":{"max_sections":4,"max_total_blocks":10,"max_blocks_per_section":3},"resource_id":"lesson","approved_items":[{"id":"8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:c7bf440d-f1da-4edc-8731-07d34d206183.i1","stem":"A student holds a book between a lamp and a wall. Which statement best explains what happens to the light that reaches the wall where the shadow forms?","card_id":"8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:c7bf440d-f1da-4edc-8731-07d34d206183","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Light from the lamp cannot reach that part of the wall because the book is in the way.","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"The book produces a dark area on the wall by sending out darkness from its cover.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"shadow-is-made-by-the-object","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"The book lets a little light leak through and creates the darker patch on the wall.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"shadow-shows-through-opaque-things","teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":null,"b":"shadow-is-made-by-the-object","c":"shadow-shows-through-opaque-things"},"correct_key":"a"},{"id":"8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:c7bf440d-f1da-4edc-8731-07d34d206183.i2","stem":"During a shadow-puppet show, a strong light shines on a puppet from the left side. Where does the puppet's shadow fall?","card_id":"8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:c7bf440d-f1da-4edc-8731-07d34d206183","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To the right of the puppet, on the side away from the light","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"To the left of the puppet, on the same side as the light","correct":false,"diagnoses":"shadow-on-same-side-as-light","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"Directly underneath the puppet, no matter where the light is placed","correct":false,"diagnoses":"shadow-on-same-side-as-light","teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":null,"b":"shadow-on-same-side-as-light","c":"shadow-on-same-side-as-light"},"correct_key":"a"},{"id":"8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:c7bf440d-f1da-4edc-8731-07d34d206183.i3","stem":"A shiny metal spoon is held under a bright lamp. What kind of shadow does the spoon cast on the table?","card_id":"8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:c7bf440d-f1da-4edc-8731-07d34d206183","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A dark shadow, because the light reflected by the shiny spoon does not pass through it.","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"A pale, weak shadow, because the shiny surface is bright and barely blocks light.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"shadow-shows-through-opaque-things","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"No shadow at all, because the spoon itself creates a bright spot instead of darkness.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"shadow-is-made-by-the-object","teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":null,"b":"shadow-shows-through-opaque-things","c":"shadow-is-made-by-the-object"},"correct_key":"a"},{"id":"8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:c7bf440d-f1da-4edc-8731-07d34d206183.i4","stem":"A flashlight is placed on the floor and aimed at a chair. The chair stands between the flashlight and the wall. Where will the chair's shadow appear?","card_id":"8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:c7bf440d-f1da-4edc-8731-07d34d206183","options":[{"key":"a","text":"On the wall behind the chair, away from the flashlight","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"On the floor under the chair, directly below it","correct":false,"diagnoses":"shadow-on-same-side-as-light","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"On the flashlight itself, because the chair sends its shadow back toward the light","correct":false,"diagnoses":"shadow-is-made-by-the-object","teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":null,"b":"shadow-on-same-side-as-light","c":"shadow-is-made-by-the-object"},"correct_key":"a"},{"id":"8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:c7bf440d-f1da-4edc-8731-07d34d206183.i5","stem":"Two students are discussing a white plastic sheet held between a lamp and a wall. The sheet blocks all light. What should they expect to see on the wall?","card_id":"8a890460-7324-4927-8a90-33aa7da5baa3:c7bf440d-f1da-4edc-8731-07d34d206183","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A dark shadow, because the sheet blocks the light from reaching the wall.","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"A light shadow that is just a slightly darker shade of white.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"shadow-shows-through-opaque-things","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"No shadow at all, because the sheet is white and reflects light instead of blocking it.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"shadow-shows-through-opaque-things","teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":null,"b":"shadow-shows-through-opaque-things","c":"shadow-shows-through-opaque-things"},"correct_key":"a"}],"misconceptions":[{"id":"shadow-is-made-by-the-object","statement":"{'id': 'shadow-is-made-by-the-object', 'description': 'The learner believes a shadow is something the object gives off or creates itself, rather than a region where light that would have reached the surface was blocked.', 'source': 'drafted'}"},{"id":"shadow-on-same-side-as-light","statement":"{'id': 'shadow-on-same-side-as-light', 'description': 'The learner expects the shadow to appear on the same side of the object as the light source, or underneath the object regardless of where the light is.', 'source': 'drafted'}"},{"id":"shadow-shows-through-opaque-things","statement":"{'id': 'shadow-shows-through-opaque-things', 'description': \"The learner thinks a shadow is just a darker copy of the object's surface colour, so a white or shiny opaque object would cast a pale or weak shadow.\", 'source': 'drafted'}"}],"prior_established":[{"id":"prior-1","statement":"light travels in straight lines"},{"id":"prior-2","statement":"Light comes from a source and travels in straight lines"},{"id":"prior-3","statement":"Light sources emit light; objects that do not emit light are seen by reflected light"},{"id":"prior-4","statement":"Examples of light sources include the Sun, lamps, torches"},{"id":"prior-5","statement":"Opaque materials block all light and form shadows"},{"id":"prior-6","statement":"Transparent materials allow most light to pass through and form little or no shadow"},{"id":"prior-7","statement":"Translucent materials allow some light to pass through and form a faint shadow"}]},"catalogue":{"version":"1.1.0","form_projection_hash":null,"teaching_projection_hash":"a9530874d702efc409dcd4c87658fe037981b8a8a6ed76e1d95a551ff8885870"}}